<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aurangabad</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Chhatrapati Sambhajinagar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aurangabad is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Founded in 1610 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-ambar">Malik Ambar</a> as Khadki, the capital of the Ahmadnagar Sultanate, it was renamed after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>, who governed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> from there and is buried nearby. It was the first capital of the Nizams of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a> until 1763, and it was officially renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhatrapati">Chhatrapati</a> Sambhajinagar in 2023.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aurangabad.jpg" alt="Aurangabad" width="320" height="215" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pranaysahu26 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bibi_Ka_Maqbara_-_The_Taj_Of_Deccan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Aurangabad stands on hilly upland in the Deccan Traps, surrounded by the Ajanta mountain range, and is the largest city of the Marathwada region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>. Paithan, the capital of the Satavahana dynasty from the 1st century BCE to the 2nd century CE, and Devagiri, the capital of the Yadava dynasty from the 9th to the 14th century, lay within the bounds of the modern district. In 1327 Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-bin-tughluq">Muhammad bin Tughluq</a> moved the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daulatabad">Daulatabad</a>, ordering a mass relocation of Delhi's population, and reversed the decision in 1334.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-ambar">Malik Ambar</a>, an Ethiopian military leader brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> as a slave who rose to be prime minister of the Ahmadnagar Sultanate, died in 1626. His son Fateh Khan renamed the city Fatehnagar, and with the capture of Devagiri Fort it passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>. In 1653 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>, then viceroy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>, made it his capital and renamed it Aurangabad. In 1681, as emperor, he moved his court there to conduct his Deccan campaigns. A city wall was built about 1683 in response to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> raids.</p><p>Although <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> did not reside there after 1684, the city stayed the main military outpost of the Mughal Deccan and a centre of trade, of embroidered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silks</a> and of Persian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> literature, with an estimated 200,000 people.</p><p>The transfer of the Nizam's capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a> in 1763 brought economic decline, and by the beginning of the nineteenth century the city was underpopulated and its buildings in decay, though it stayed the second city of the Nizam's dominions. The British set up a cantonment outside it in 1816, and its first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> mill opened in 1889. In 1960 Aurangabad and the Marathwada region became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The city is called the City of Gates for the 52 gates built in the Mughal era, some of which still stand. Bibi Ka Maqbara, the mausoleum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>'s wife Dilras Banu Begum, imitates the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taj-mahal">Taj Mahal</a>. The Panchakki is a seventeenth-century watermill fed by an underground channel more than 8 km long. The Ajanta and Ellora caves on the outskirts have been <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Sites</a> since 1983. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a> is the official language, followed in use by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> and Hindi.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aurangabad” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He received the title Asaf Jah, which gave the dynasty its name, and made <b>Aurangabad</b> his capital.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hyderabad-state">Hyderabad State · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the capital fell, the Ethiopian-born minister Malik Ambar kept the state alive from <b>Khadki</b> (later Aurangabad), pioneering guerrilla warfare and a land revenue survey later adopted by the Mughals.</blockquote><a href="/state/ahmadnagar-sultanate">Ahmadnagar Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
